WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.400
The deaths of beloved TV characters&nbsp;
can shock viewers like nothing else.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:08.560
They might feel sudden, but they've&nbsp;
often been planned years in advance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:12.960
So we're here to examine those characters&nbsp;
who were doomed from episode one.

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:18.320
Premiering on Starz in 2014, the&nbsp;
crime drama Power follows nightclub&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:24.160
owner and drug kingpin James "Ghost" St.&nbsp;
Patrick. He's a family man and entrepreneur&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:28.400
who poses as a pillar of the community on&nbsp;
the strength of his rags-to-riches façade.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:33.680
Secretly, his best friend is his partner in&nbsp;
literal crime, and the first season sees him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:36.960
embark upon an extramarital affair&nbsp;
with his middle school sweetheart.

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:42.560
Ghost is brought to life by actor Omari Hardwick,&nbsp;
who entices viewers with his sharp suits,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:48.320
menacing focus, and chiseled good looks. Ghost's&nbsp;
secret life of crime is destined to catch up to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:54.800
him in the end, though. In the final season, he's&nbsp;
shot dead by his son Tariq. As it turns out, Power&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.800 --> 00:01:00.720
creator Courtney A. Kemp always planned to have&nbsp;
Ghost die. As she wrote in the Los Angeles Times,

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:04.560
"The series is about consequences for&nbsp;
their actions. The characters always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:06.880
cause their own deaths, one way or another."

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:13.040
Sons of Anarchy premiered on FX in 2008,&nbsp;
hooking fans with its killer characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:17.840
and gruesome scenes of gun violence and gang&nbsp;
battles, and lead character Jax Teller quickly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:23.520
became beloved by fans. As the prodigal son who's&nbsp;
set to take charge of the California motorcycle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:28.960
crew his father once led, he must manage his&nbsp;
overbearing mother, life as a new father,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:34.560
and his own torn emotions over the one who&nbsp;
got away. Over the course of seven seasons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:39.120
he works to transform the trajectory of his&nbsp;
crew so that violence and criminal activity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:43.920
are no longer at the forefront of their lives.&nbsp;
During this time, he and his loved ones endure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:49.200
plenty of terrifying struggles. In the end, he&nbsp;
sacrifices himself for the well-being of the crew.

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:54.800
This sounds like a lot for writers to put a&nbsp;
character through, but in a 2014 interview&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:59.920
with E!, executive producer Paris Barclay made&nbsp;
it clear that this was always the plan for Jax.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:01.520
As he put it,

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:05.440
"I knew that he wasn't going to end up at&nbsp;
a carnival buying snow cones for his sons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:09.280
and that was the last shot. I always&nbsp;
knew this was going to end badly."

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:11.280
"What happens at the end of the day?"

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:17.120
"The bad guys lose."

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:24.000
In 2018, the Sons of Anarchy legacy continued&nbsp;
with the premiere of the spinoff Mayans M.C.,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:26.720
which centers on a tight-knit&nbsp;
motorcycle crew in Mexico.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:32.320
It remains to be seen if the lead actor on that&nbsp;
show will also experience a shocking demise.

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:38.480
Michael K. Williams is a phenomenal actor&nbsp;
who often portrays conflicted individuals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:43.520
who tend to overshadow the protagonists.&nbsp;
From Chalky White on Boardwalk Empire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:48.640
to Montrose Freeman on Lovecraft Country, he&nbsp;
seamlessly transforms into dark and enigmatic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:54.640
characters that audiences grow to love. On the&nbsp;
HBO series The Wire, he played Omar Little, a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.640 --> 00:02:59.840
stick-up guy who robs drug dealers and keeps them&nbsp;
on their toes. The Baltimore-set crime drama has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:04.560
been hailed as a classic and frequently cited as&nbsp;
one of the greatest shows in television history.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:09.840
Omar stands out as an especially great part of&nbsp;
the series, thanks to the strategic way he runs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:15.680
the streets, his well-rounded moral compass,&nbsp;
and his unique status as a gay man. TV viewers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:20.960
in the early 2000s had truly never before seen a&nbsp;
queer character like Omar on a gritty crime show.

00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:26.960
In the final season, Omar is killed in a&nbsp;
convenience store by a young drug dealer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:31.600
In 2008, Williams shared his feelings&nbsp;
about his character's death with MTV News,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:35.840
as he revealed that he'd always known&nbsp;
it was going to happen. As he put it,

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:41.120
"It took me for a loop. I was also saddened. Not&nbsp;
that he was dying, 'cause when I got the job,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:46.000
they told me season one Omar was only going&nbsp;
to be around for seven episodes. 'After that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:52.400
expect a bullet.' It still hurt. Five years, you&nbsp;
lock with this dude, you know the dude real well."

00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:53.920
He added,

00:03:53.920 --> 00:03:58.560
"The people of Baltimore, I got to know&nbsp;
the streets, how they walk, how they eat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:04.240
how they dress. I soaked all of that up. I&nbsp;
feel like I lost one of my best friends."

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:06.560
"A man got to have a code."

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:12.240
In House of Cards' first season, newspaper&nbsp;
journalist Zoe Barnes is the key to Frank&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.240 --> 00:04:18.080
Underwood's demise. Underwood holds many secrets.&nbsp;
Some have the power to destroy his growing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.080 --> 00:04:23.760
political career, while others could destroy&nbsp;
his calculated marriage. Zoe is also seduced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:28.800
by the criminal congressman. As she learns more&nbsp;
about him, she shares her intel with her boss,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:34.560
but she also grows fearful as Frank begins to&nbsp;
stalk and antagonize her. He ultimately follows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:40.640
her into a subway station and pushes her onto the&nbsp;
tracks. Her death is swift, cold, and shocking.

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:47.360
House of Cards executive producer David Fincher&nbsp;
usually employs a dark visual style to his films,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.360 --> 00:04:53.440
so perhaps it's no surprise that his foray into&nbsp;
TV followed suit. Nevertheless, fans were blown&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.440 --> 00:04:58.640
away by the sinister scene. When series creator&nbsp;
Beau Willimon spoke with The Hollywood Reporter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.640 --> 00:05:04.720
in 2014, he revealed that Zoe's death had been&nbsp;
planned from the beginning. As he explained,

00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:06.480
"We told Kate Mara from the get-go.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:11.120
I always knew that Zoe was going to meet her&nbsp;
maker in the first episode of season two."

00:05:11.760 --> 00:05:12.960
He later added,

00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:16.000
"She is so wonderful that as&nbsp;
we got closer to doing it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:19.400
we reconsidered for a second,&nbsp;
then we stuck to our guts."

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:25.440
Amazon's The Boys explores an alternate universe&nbsp;
in which people with extraordinary gifts are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:30.880
labeled "Supes," and attract the attention of&nbsp;
conglomerates. The powerful corporation Vought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.880 --> 00:05:35.760
International has created a team of celebrity&nbsp;
Supes called the Seven, who fight crime and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.760 --> 00:05:41.120
work to save humanity. But this work is actually&nbsp;
a façade. When the cameras and capes are off,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.120 --> 00:05:45.360
many members of the Seven lead challenging&nbsp;
lives, and are even dangers to humanity.

00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:51.280
Take for example, Lamplighter, a former member&nbsp;
of the Seven played by Shawn Ashmore who has the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.280 --> 00:05:56.560
ability to set things ablaze. He's mentioned&nbsp;
quite a bit in season one, though he doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.560 --> 00:06:02.480
actually appear until season two. The truth about&nbsp;
his complex allegiances is eventually revealed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.480 --> 00:06:07.920
and then he sets himself on fire. After&nbsp;
the episode aired, Ashmore told TV Line,

00:06:07.920 --> 00:06:11.920
"It was such a bold choice. The way that I&nbsp;
look at death scenes is if you’re gonna go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.920 --> 00:06:16.080
out on a show or on a film, it better&nbsp;
be good, and it better be impactful."

00:06:16.960 --> 00:06:22.400
When Lost premiered on ABC in 2004, it seemed&nbsp;
fairly straightforward, as a group of plane&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.400 --> 00:06:27.120
crash survivors came together to figure out life&nbsp;
on a deserted island. But over the course of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.120 --> 00:06:32.560
its six-season run, it became clear over and&nbsp;
over again that it was anything but simple.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.280 --> 00:06:37.920
There are supernatural elements to the island,&nbsp;
and every survivor has a complicated past that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:42.480
led them there. Ana Lucia Cortez, for&nbsp;
one, was on the plane's tail section&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.480 --> 00:06:48.480
and emerged as a leader in season two. She puts&nbsp;
her LAPD skills to use on the island, but she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.480 --> 00:06:53.520
also gains a lot of enemies in doing so. She's&nbsp;
eventually killed by fellow survivor Michael.

00:06:54.400 --> 00:06:58.880
In a 2006 interview with TV Guide,&nbsp;
Lost showrunners Damon Lindelof&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.880 --> 00:07:04.000
and Carlton Cuse discussed details about Ana&nbsp;
Lucia's arc. As they developed the character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.000 --> 00:07:07.600
and put the casting word out, they heard&nbsp;
from Michelle Rodriguez's agent that she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.600 --> 00:07:12.400
was interested in the role, but only&nbsp;
for one season. As Lindelof recalled,

00:07:12.400 --> 00:07:15.680
"She wanted to do one kick-ass&nbsp;
arc, as she described it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.680 --> 00:07:19.520
and we basically started to wrap our&nbsp;
brains around her energy and say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.520 --> 00:07:22.880
'Yeah, we'll bring you on the show and then&nbsp;
we'll kill you off at the end of the year.'"

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:25.520
"I can't do this anymore."

00:07:26.720 --> 00:07:32.720
When Tara Knowles pops up on Sons of Anarchy in&nbsp;
Charming, California, Jax Teller is stunned. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.720 --> 00:07:38.160
loved each other in high school, but Tara left to&nbsp;
focus on her studies. She comes back as a doctor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.160 --> 00:07:42.240
finding Jax in pretty bad shape, with&nbsp;
a drug-addicted girlfriend and a new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.240 --> 00:07:47.280
baby. Despite their hiccups and hurdles, the&nbsp;
sweethearts manage to stick it out, as they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.280 --> 00:07:52.640
get married and have a son of their own. But Tara&nbsp;
is always at odds with Jax's overbearing mother,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.640 --> 00:07:58.080
Gemma. For her part, Gemma is wary of Tara's&nbsp;
true intentions, while also desiring to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.080 --> 00:08:02.880
maintain her position as the most important&nbsp;
woman in her son's life. She ends up killing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.880 --> 00:08:08.720
Tara while acting on false information. Jax is&nbsp;
devastated and never recovers from the pain.

00:08:09.360 --> 00:08:13.840
Katey Sagal, who played Gemma and is married&nbsp;
to show creator Kurt Sutter, had hoped that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.840 --> 00:08:18.160
he would change his mind about Tara's death.&nbsp;
But Sutter stood firm, having decided that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.160 --> 00:08:22.800
Jax needed the major emotional shift that this&nbsp;
provided as the series approached its ending.

00:08:24.240 --> 00:08:28.880
Over the course of Lost's six seasons, viewers&nbsp;
discover that Jack Shephard is more than the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.880 --> 00:08:34.080
self-appointed leader of the survivors. He's&nbsp;
also been connected to them for years. Through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.080 --> 00:08:38.560
flashbacks, these links are revealed, as are&nbsp;
the shameful mistakes of his past that led&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.560 --> 00:08:44.480
him to Oceanic Flight 815. He grapples with&nbsp;
his estranged relationship with his father,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.480 --> 00:08:49.040
his love for Kate, and his animosity for&nbsp;
Sawyer, who challenges Jack's leadership.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.600 --> 00:08:55.040
Early versions of Lost actually killed Jack in&nbsp;
the pilot and made Kate the group's leader. As&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.040 --> 00:08:58.960
critic Alan Sepinwall wrote in his&nbsp;
book The Revolution Was Televised,

00:08:58.960 --> 00:09:03.040
"In the vein of Alfred Hitchcock's Psycho [...]&nbsp;
they planned to pull the rug out from under the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.040 --> 00:09:08.080
audience by killing Jack midway through the&nbsp;
first episode, forcing Kate to take charge."

00:09:08.080 --> 00:09:12.960
Of course, Jack instead lives and gets off the&nbsp;
island, only to return with other survivors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.960 --> 00:09:17.840
who made it home. The big mystery of the show&nbsp;
ends up being whether the survivors are dead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.840 --> 00:09:23.200
existing in an alternate universe, or&nbsp;
experiencing some kind of strange shared dream.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.200 --> 00:09:28.400
And ultimately, Jack does end up biting the&nbsp;
bullet, just much later than originally planned.

00:09:29.520 --> 00:09:35.200
The FX drama Pose is loud, proud, and&nbsp;
unapologetic. Set in New York beginning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.200 --> 00:09:40.320
in the late 80s, it follows the lives of gay and&nbsp;
transgender people who participate in the city's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.320 --> 00:09:45.440
underground ballroom scene, brought to life by&nbsp;
a cast that consists of multiple trans actors.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.440 --> 00:09:50.880
In 2019, co-creator Ryan Murphy and producer&nbsp;
Janet Mock talked with Deadline about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.880 --> 00:09:55.040
decision to have fan favorite Candy&nbsp;
Ferocity killed in the second season.

00:09:55.040 --> 00:09:59.920
Played by Angelica Ross, Candy is a member&nbsp;
of the House of Ferocity who's often mocked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.920 --> 00:10:05.600
by ballroom announcer Pray Tell. But she's true to&nbsp;
her name as a ferocious force to be reckoned with,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.600 --> 00:10:10.080
and her death is deeply felt in&nbsp;
the community. Mock told Deadline,

00:10:10.080 --> 00:10:14.160
"Our hardest thing was which character&nbsp;
[to kill]. So once we figured out which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.160 --> 00:10:18.320
character [...] we kind of plotted the whole&nbsp;
first half of the season around losing Candy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.320 --> 00:10:20.000
and really showing what that would look like."

00:10:20.800 --> 00:10:25.520
The decision was necessary to the story as they&nbsp;
had built it, but Mock and Murphy also felt it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.520 --> 00:10:30.480
was important to shed light on the violence that&nbsp;
trans women were facing then and still face today.

00:10:31.360 --> 00:10:33.440
"We will never again get the opportunity to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.600 --> 00:10:40.880
tell her what she meant to us, to&nbsp;
thank her for what she gave us."

00:10:41.920 --> 00:10:47.760
On the British period drama Downton Abbey, Lady&nbsp;
Sybil Crawley, as played by Jessica Brown-Findlay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.760 --> 00:10:52.640
is the youngest daughter of Robert and Cora&nbsp;
Crawley, the Earl and Countess of Grantham.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.640 --> 00:10:57.360
Along with her very different sisters, she&nbsp;
maneuvers through high society and the complicated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.360 --> 00:11:02.160
social pressures that come with her position.&nbsp;
She makes great efforts to set herself apart,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.160 --> 00:11:06.880
intermingling with classes deemed lesser than&nbsp;
her own. She even falls in love with an Irish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.880 --> 00:11:12.000
driver named Tom, spurning the turned-up&nbsp;
noses of her aristocratic family. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.000 --> 00:11:17.440
couple are able to welcome a daughter, but Sybil&nbsp;
tragically dies from childbirth complications.

00:11:17.440 --> 00:11:22.000
Worried about being stuck on a never-ending&nbsp;
series, Brown-Findlay set her terms well before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.000 --> 00:11:27.600
the show began. As Downton Abbey creator&nbsp;
Julian Fellowes told Vanity Fair in 2015,

00:11:27.600 --> 00:11:30.640
"Jessica had said she was going&nbsp;
to leave right from the beginning.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.640 --> 00:11:35.040
She said, 'I'm doing three years, then&nbsp;
I'm leaving.' So that was all worked out."

00:11:35.680 --> 00:11:37.600
"Is there anything we should do, Mr. Carson?"

00:11:39.680 --> 00:11:44.400
"Carry on, Daisy, as we all must."

